    EditorialLessonRecord("Class 9–10|Class 9 Matter and Mixtures|Evaporation and diffusion",
        "explicit topic alias",
        "A liquid has fixed volume but no fixed shape because its particles remain close while moving past one another.\n\nGases have neither a fixed shape nor a fixed volume.",
        "Intermolecular attractions give liquids measurable viscosity, surface tension and vapour pressure.\n\nGas particles are far apart, move rapidly in random directions and have weak attractions, so a gas fills its container and is easily compressed.",
        "water flows to match a container but remains nearly incompressible because its molecules are close together.\n\nExample: perfume molecules spread through a room by diffusion, showing that gas particles move and mix.",
        "liquid properties depend on intermolecular forces and temperature\n\nfar-apart fast particles -> diffusion + high compressibility",
        "Liquids are not particles with no attraction; their particles are close and interact strongly enough to maintain volume.\n\nA gas does not have zero mass; its low density simply makes its mass less obvious.",
        "Liquid properties matter in fuels, lubricants, medicines, coatings and fluid transport.\n\nGas behavior is used in breathing equipment, tyres, refrigeration and weather forecasting.",
        listOf("water flows to match a container but remains nearly incompressible because its molecules are close together.","Example: perfume molecules spread through a room by diffusion, showing that gas particles move and mix.","liquid properties depend on intermolecular forces and temperature\n\nfar-apart fast particles -> diffusion + high compressibility","Intermolecular attractions give liquids measurable viscosity, surface tension and vapour pressure.","Gas particles are far apart, move rapidly in random directions and have weak attractions, so a gas fills its container and is easily compressed.","Liquids are not particles with no attraction; their particles are close and interact strongly enough to maintain volume.\n\nA gas does not have zero mass; its low density simply makes its mass less obvious."),
        listOf("Intermolecular forces","Viscosity","Vapour pressure","Diffusion","Gas pressure","Expansion","Liquids","Gases")),
    EditorialLessonRecord("Class 9–10|Class 9 Matter and Mixtures|Sublimation and latent heat",
        "explicit topic alias",
        "Sublimation separates a solid that changes directly to vapour from non-subliming solids.\n\nHeat is energy transferred solely because of a temperature difference.",
        "Heat the mixture gently under an inverted funnel or cold surface. The sublimable component forms vapour and deposits as solid on the cooler surface, while the nonvolatile component remains. Suitable examples include ammonium chloride, camphor or naphthalene mixed with sand or salt.\n\nHeat flows spontaneously from hotter to colder regions. In chemistry, q is positive when the system absorbs heat and negative when it releases heat.",
        "On gently heating ammonium chloride mixed with sodium chloride, ammonium chloride deposits on the cool funnel while sodium chloride remains in the dish.\n\nExample: if a reaction transfers 5.0 kJ to a water bath, qsystem = -5.0 kJ and qsurroundings = +5.0 kJ.",
        "solid --heat--> vapour --cooling--> solid deposit\n\nqsystem = -qsurroundings in an insulated calorimeter",
        "Do not call ordinary evaporation sublimation; sublimation begins with a solid and bypasses the liquid state under the stated conditions.\n\nHeat is energy in transit, not a substance stored inside a body; internal energy is the state property.",
        "Sublimation is used to purify volatile solids and illustrates particle motion during direct solid-gas changes.\n\nHeat-transfer calculations guide reactors, refrigeration, climate control and material processing.",
        listOf("On gently heating ammonium chloride mixed with sodium chloride, ammonium chloride deposits on the cool funnel while sodium chloride remains in the dish.","Example: if a reaction transfers 5.0 kJ to a water bath, qsystem = -5.0 kJ and qsurroundings = +5.0 kJ.","solid --heat--> vapour --cooling--> solid deposit\n\nqsystem = -qsurroundings in an insulated calorimeter","Heat the mixture gently under an inverted funnel or cold surface.","The sublimable component forms vapour and deposits as solid on the cooler surface, while the nonvolatile component remains.","Suitable examples include ammonium chloride, camphor or naphthalene mixed with sand or salt."),
        listOf("Changes of state","Deposition","Volatility","Temperature","Calorimetry","Internal energy","Sublimation as a separation method","Heat")),
    EditorialLessonRecord("Class 9–10|Class 9 Matter and Mixtures|Solutions, suspensions and colloids",
        "explicit topic alias",
        "Solutions can be classified by physical state, solvent-solute combination and degree of saturation.\n\nA colloid contains dispersed particles intermediate in size between a true solution and a suspension.",
        "They may be gaseous, liquid or solid and may be unsaturated, saturated or supersaturated. A true solution is homogeneous at the particle scale.\n\nColloidal particles remain distributed through Brownian motion and surface charge and scatter light in the Tyndall effect.",
        "air is a gas solution, salt water is a solid-in-liquid solution and brass is a solid solution.\n\nExample: milk is a colloidal dispersion whose droplets scatter a beam of light even though they do not rapidly settle.",
        "solution = solvent + one or more solutes\n\ndispersed phase + dispersion medium -> colloid",
        "A solution does not have to be a liquid, and a suspension is not a true homogeneous solution.\n\nA colloid may look homogeneous to the eye but is heterogeneous at the microscopic scale.",
        "Classifying solutions helps select preparation, storage and separation methods.\n\nColloids occur in foods, medicines, paints, smoke control and nanomaterial processing.",
        listOf("air is a gas solution, salt water is a solid-in-liquid solution and brass is a solid solution.","Example: milk is a colloidal dispersion whose droplets scatter a beam of light even though they do not rapidly settle.","solution = solvent + one or more solutes\n\ndispersed phase + dispersion medium -> colloid","They may be gaseous, liquid or solid and may be unsaturated, saturated or supersaturated.","A true solution is homogeneous at the particle scale.","Colloidal particles remain distributed through Brownian motion and surface charge and scatter light in the Tyndall effect."),
        listOf("Solvent and solute","Saturation","Homogeneous mixtures","Tyndall effect","Brownian motion","Coagulation","Types","Colloids")),
    EditorialLessonRecord("Class 9–10|Class 9 Matter and Mixtures|Tyndall effect and concentration",
        "new case",
        "The Tyndall effect is visible light scattering by dispersed particles and can distinguish some colloids from true solutions.",
        "Scattering depends on particle size, refractive-index contrast and concentration. A visible light path is not a direct quantitative concentration reading without calibration and controlled conditions.",
        "A narrow light beam is visible through a suitable milk-water colloid but not similarly through a clean sodium chloride true solution. Both mixtures can appear uniformly mixed to the unaided eye.",
        "colloidal particles scatter light more strongly than molecularly dispersed solutes",
        "A transparent appearance does not prove that a sample is a pure substance, and scattering alone does not identify the solute.",
        "Light scattering helps characterize dispersions and distinguish forms of mixtures.",
        listOf("A narrow light beam is visible through a suitable milk-water colloid but not similarly through a clean sodium chloride true solution.","Both mixtures can appear uniformly mixed to the unaided eye.","colloidal particles scatter light more strongly than molecularly dispersed solutes","Scattering depends on particle size, refractive-index contrast and concentration.","A visible light path is not a direct quantitative concentration reading without calibration and controlled conditions.","A transparent appearance does not prove that a sample is a pure substance, and scattering alone does not identify the solute."),
        listOf("colloidal particles scatter light more strongly than molecularly dispersed solutes","The Tyndall effect is visible light scattering by dispersed particles and can distinguish some colloids from true solutions.")),
    EditorialLessonRecord("Class 9–10|Class 9 Matter and Mixtures|Separating mixtures in class 9",
        "explicit topic alias",
        "Centrifugation speeds separation by spinning a mixture so denser suspended particles move outward and settle faster.\n\nPaper chromatography separates dissolved substances because they travel differently with a solvent over paper.\n\nSublimation separates a solid that changes directly to vapour from non-subliming solids.",
        "Opposing tubes must have equal masses and be placed symmetrically. After spinning at the specified speed and time, the dense sediment is the pellet and the liquid above it is the supernatant. Centrifugation helps when particles are too small or slow to settle by gravity alone.\n\nPlace a small sample spot on a pencil baseline above the solvent. The solvent rises by capillary action; components repeatedly partition between the mobile solvent and stationary water/paper environment. Mark the solvent front immediately and compare spots using Rf under identical conditions.\n\nHeat the mixture gently under an inverted funnel or cold surface. The sublimable component forms vapour and deposits as solid on the cooler surface, while the nonvolatile component remains. Suitable examples include ammonium chloride, camphor or naphthalene mixed with sand or salt.",
        "A milk separator uses spinning to divide components of different density; a laboratory centrifuge can separate fine chalk from water into pellet and supernatant.\n\nExample: If an ink dye travels 3.0 cm while the solvent front travels 5.0 cm, Rf = 3.0/5.0 = 0.60.\n\nExample: On gently heating ammonium chloride mixed with sodium chloride, ammonium chloride deposits on the cool funnel while sodium chloride remains in the dish.",
        "greater density difference and rotation -> faster sedimentation\n\nRf = distance travelled by solute / distance travelled by solvent front; 0 <= Rf <= 1\n\nsolid --heat--> vapour --cooling--> solid deposit",
        "Never run a centrifuge with unbalanced tubes, an unsecured lid or cracked containers; imbalance can damage the rotor and injure users.\n\nDo not draw the baseline in ink or immerse the sample spot in solvent; either action contaminates or washes away the starting sample.\n\nDo not call ordinary evaporation sublimation; sublimation begins with a solid and bypasses the liquid state under the stated conditions.",
        "Centrifugation is used in dairies, clinical blood processing, wastewater treatment and laboratory sample preparation.\n\nChromatography separates inks, food colours, plant pigments and components used in forensic or quality-control comparisons.\n\nSublimation is used to purify volatile solids and illustrates particle motion during direct solid-gas changes.",
        listOf("A milk separator uses spinning to divide components of different density","a laboratory centrifuge can separate fine chalk from water into pellet and supernatant.","Example: If an ink dye travels 3.0 cm while the solvent front travels 5.0 cm, Rf = 3.0/5.0 = 0.60.","Example: On gently heating ammonium chloride mixed with sodium chloride, ammonium chloride deposits on the cool funnel while sodium chloride remains in the dish.","greater density difference and rotation -> faster sedimentation\n\nRf = distance travelled by solute / distance travelled by solvent front; 0 <= Rf <= 1\n\nsolid --heat--> vapour --cooling--> solid deposit","Opposing tubes must have equal masses and be placed symmetrically."),
        listOf("Density","Sedimentation","Laboratory safety","Capillary action","Solubility","Rf values","Changes of state","Deposition","Volatility","Centrifugation of mixtures","Paper chromatography","Sublimation as a separation method")),
    EditorialLessonRecord("Class 9–10|Class 9 Matter and Mixtures|Crystallisation of dissolved solids",
        "existing authored record",
        "Crystallisation purifies a dissolved solid by forming ordered crystals from a hot concentrated solution as it cools.",
        "Choose a solvent in which the solute is much more soluble when hot than cold. Dissolve the impure solid in minimum hot solvent, hot-filter insoluble impurities, concentrate if needed, cool without disturbance, filter the crystals, wash with a little cold solvent and dry. Soluble impurities largely remain in the mother liquor.",
        "Copper sulfate containing insoluble dirt is dissolved in hot water and filtered. Blue crystals form on slow cooling while much of the soluble impurity stays in solution.",
        "hot saturated solution -> cooling -> crystals + mother liquor",
        "Do not evaporate all the solvent to dryness: rapid complete evaporation can trap impurities and gives poorer crystals.",
        "Crystallisation purifies salt, sugar, pharmaceuticals and laboratory reagents while offering better purity than evaporation alone.",
        listOf("Copper sulfate containing insoluble dirt is dissolved in hot water and filtered.","Blue crystals form on slow cooling while much of the soluble impurity stays in solution.","hot saturated solution -> cooling -> crystals + mother liquor","Choose a solvent in which the solute is much more soluble when hot than cold.","Dissolve the impure solid in minimum hot solvent, hot-filter insoluble impurities, concentrate if needed, cool without disturbance, filter the crystals, wash with a little cold solvent and dry.","Soluble impurities largely remain in the mother liquor."),
        listOf("Solubility","Saturation","Filtration")),
    EditorialLessonRecord("Class 9–10|Class 9 Matter and Mixtures|Simple distillation of liquid mixtures",
        "existing authored record",
        "Simple distillation separates a volatile liquid from a nonvolatile solute or from another liquid with a sufficiently different boiling point.",
        "Heat the mixture safely, allow the more volatile component to vaporise, cool the vapour in a condenser and collect the distillate. A thermometer measures vapour temperature near the condenser entrance. Anti-bumping measures and an open receiving path prevent sudden boiling and pressure buildup.",
        "Distilling salt water produces liquid water as distillate while dissolved salt remains in the flask; filtration could not remove that dissolved salt.",
        "liquid mixture --heating--> vapour --condensation--> distillate",
        "Do not seal a distillation apparatus or heat a flask to complete dryness, and do not confuse distillation with simple evaporation because the solvent is recovered.",
        "Distillation supports water purification, solvent recovery and preparation of distilled laboratory water.",
        listOf("Distilling salt water produces liquid water as distillate while dissolved salt remains in the flask","filtration could not remove that dissolved salt.","liquid mixture --heating--> vapour --condensation--> distillate","Heat the mixture safely, allow the more volatile component to vaporise, cool the vapour in a condenser and collect the distillate.","A thermometer measures vapour temperature near the condenser entrance.","Anti-bumping measures and an open receiving path prevent sudden boiling and pressure buildup."),
